    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently working on a formulation for a low-fat mayonnaise variant that uses a reduced lipid profile. We're seeing significant phase separation issues during accelerated stability testing, specifically in high-acid environments (pH < 3.8). Could you explain the molecular mechanism of how lecithin stabilizes the oil-water interface in these conditions, and how the presence of acetic acid might interfere with the interfacial film stability?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin stabilizes the emulsion by adsorbing onto the oil-water interface, where its amphiphilic structure with hydrophobic tails embedded in the oil and hydrophilic heads facing the water forms a protective barrier. This barrier physically prevents the droplets from coalescing under shear or thermodynamic stress. In high-acid environments, acetic acid can interfere by potentially protonating the phosphate groups on the lecithin head, reducing its net negative charge. This decrease in electrostatic repulsion between stabilizing molecules can weaken the interfacial film, making the emulsion more susceptible to phase separation.",
      "source": "target",
