    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. If the electrostatic repulsion is dropping because of the protonation, would increasing the concentration of lecithin mitigate the instability, or would that just create issues with the final texture? Also, are there any other surfactants you'd recommend to reinforce that interfacial film against the acid?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Increasing lecithin concentration might delay instability by providing more interfacial coverage, but it could indeed compromise the texture by potentially leading to a greasy mouthfeel or increased viscosity. To reinforce the film, consider adding mono- and diglycerides, which can offer steric stabilization alongside the existing electrostatic repulsion. Furthermore, using a small amount of a food-grade polymer, like xanthan gum, dissolved in the water phase can help control the continuous phase viscosity, providing additional mechanical stability against separation.",
      "source": "target",
